## Requirements
- Create a short link for a long URL, and redirect from the short link to it.
- Links can have a custom alias and an expiry date.
- 100 million new links per month, and 100 times more reads than writes.
- Redirects in under 50 ms at the 99th percentile. Availability matters more than strict consistency.

## Capacity estimates
Table: Back-of-envelope numbers
| Quantity | Estimate | How |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Writes | about 40 per second | 100 million per month, 2.6 million seconds in a month |
| Reads | about 4,000 per second | 100 times the writes |
| Storage over 5 years | about 3 TB | 6 billion links at 500 bytes each |

## API
Table: Three calls
| Call | What it does |
| --- | --- |
| POST /links | Body: url, optional alias and expires_at. Returns the short code. |
| GET /{code} | Redirects to the long URL. |
| DELETE /links/{code} | Removes a link. |

## High-level design
```graph type=flowchart dir=TB title="URL shortener"
client [client]: Browser
lb [loadbalancer]: Load balancer
api [service]: API service
cache [cache]: Redis
db [datastore]: Link store
ids [service]: ID generator
client -> lb
lb -> api
api -> cache: look up code
api -.-> db: on a cache miss
api -> ids: new code (writes)
api -> db: save link (writes)
```

## Data model
Table: The links table
| Column | Type | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| code | string, 7 characters | primary key, base62 of an id |
| url | text | the long URL |
| created_at | timestamp | |
| expires_at | timestamp | optional |

## Deep dives
- **Codes.** An ID generator hands each API server a block of 1,000 ids, so no coordination is needed per request. The id is written in base62: seven characters give $62^7$, about 3.5 trillion codes.
- **Reads.** Hot links are kept in Redis with a time to live. A miss reads the link store and fills the cache.

## Trade-offs and decisions
> [!decision] Redirect status
> Use 302, not 301. Browsers do not cache a 302, so every click reaches us and can be counted. The cost is more traffic.

> [!tradeoff] Counter, not a hash
> A counter never collides and is predictable, so codes can be guessed. Random-looking codes need a check for collisions on every write.

## Open questions
- [ ] Abuse: should URLs be scanned before they are shortened?
- [ ] Custom aliases: which words are reserved?
